  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and the rule is enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`, index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has time field `timestamp`. Its fields include `@timestamp`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `response` (text, with a `response.keyword` subfield), `url`, `host`, `geo.*`, and others.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-7b6fda48 Web logs' (id `db2af47f-a7f4-4ca7-a338-467a6e725eb0`) has exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of count by date histogram on `timestamp`.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart of count by terms on `response.keyword`.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-7b6fda48 5xx errors' (id `80064b51-5b12-4be1-905e-3039a67ba2cc`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, scheduled every 1m. It counts documents in a 5-minute window and fires when the count is greater than 10. The query is a regexp on `response.keyword` matching `5[0-9][0-9]`, which covers 500\u2013599. It has no connectors. Its last run succeeded with 0 alerts.\n\n4. **Panel rename and read-back:** The response-code panel is now titled 'Responses by code', and the dashboard still has two panels. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`. The rule was created enabled, so no enable call was needed.\n\nI did not check the distribution of `response.keyword` values, because I had no endpoint to run an ad-hoc search. The regexp is anchored, so it only matches three-digit 5xx codes.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
